WEBVTT

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:02.319
You open a shop’s website.

00:00:02.319 --> 00:00:03.663
A banner pops up.

00:00:03.663 --> 00:00:06.209
Accept all, reject all, or manage.

00:00:06.319 --> 00:00:08.490
Before you tap,
what is a cookie?

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:11.746
It’s a small note a website
asks your browser to keep.

00:00:11.746 --> 00:00:14.575
Next visit, your browser
hands the note back.

00:00:14.733 --> 00:00:16.823
Some notes do a job you asked for.

00:00:16.823 --> 00:00:18.269
Add a lamp to your basket,

00:00:18.269 --> 00:00:20.731
and the shop remembers it
as you browse.

00:00:20.731 --> 00:00:22.759
It’s the same when you stay logged in.

00:00:22.759 --> 00:00:26.333
Essential notes like this usually
don’t need your permission.

00:00:26.538 --> 00:00:29.272
Other notes are about you,
not your basket.

00:00:29.272 --> 00:00:31.718
Analytics cookies help a site
count visits,

00:00:31.718 --> 00:00:33.796
and see which pages you read.

00:00:33.796 --> 00:00:36.703
Advertising cookies can come
from another company,

00:00:36.703 --> 00:00:38.859
whose code sits on many websites.

00:00:38.859 --> 00:00:41.707
It can recognize your browser
from site to site.

00:00:41.707 --> 00:00:44.774
That’s why the lamp follows you
onto a news website.

00:00:45.027 --> 00:00:48.433
So, “Accept all” usually means
yes to these extras.

00:00:48.433 --> 00:00:50.932
“Reject all” usually keeps the essentials.

00:00:50.932 --> 00:00:52.740
Your basket still works.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:54.492
Options differ on every site,

00:00:54.492 --> 00:00:57.550
so “Manage” shows
what’s really on the list.

00:00:57.803 --> 00:00:59.966
A cookie can remember your basket.

00:00:59.966 --> 00:01:01.549
Or it can remember you.
